    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d465">

                <pb facs="0470" n="380" sameAs="frus:frus1918Russiav02/pg380"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 861.00/2703</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Irkutsk</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Harris</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d465-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Irkutsk</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1918-09-14"><hi rendition="#italic">September 14,
                        1918</hi></date>. <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 11.57
                        p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>85. Referring my No. 81 [82?], 13th. Golovachev will probably visit Peking and
                    advocate the recognition of Omsk government. I <pb facs="0471" n="381"
                        xml:id="pg381"/> have to suggest great caution in dealing with this
                    question. Such recognition would immediately estrange Samara, Orenburg, and
                    Ekaterinburg. Government [conference] now convened at Ufa in the attempt to form
                    all-Russian government. Omsk government reluctantly sent delegates at late date
                    to attend Ufa conference.</p>
                <closer>[<signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Harris</hi></signed>]</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d465-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d465-f1-content">
                <p>Sent via the Legation in China.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
